Ada Spawn Manager
=================

The Ada spawn manager implements the concept described in the GNAT GPL
package System.OS_Lib. It is used to solve the problem of spawning
processes at arbitrary times from multitasking Ada programs:

[quote, GNAT COMPILER COMPONENTS, SYSTEM.OS_LIB specification]
____
If the parent is using tasking, and needs to spawn subprocesses at arbitrary
times, one technique is for the parent to spawn (very early) a particular
spawn-manager subprocess whose job is to spawn other processes. The
spawn-manager avoids tasking. The parent sends messages to the spawn-manager
requesting it to spawn processes, using whatever inter-process communication
mechanism you like, such as sockets.
____

The Ada spawn manager uses UNIX domain sockets provided by the Anet library to
implement fast and simple IPC.

Process execution
-----------------
The existing public command-string API deliberately retains Bash semantics.
The manager normalizes each request to an absolute `/bin/bash -o pipefail -c`
launch and sends it through the same internal process representation intended
for structured argument-vector requests. The command-string procedure maps an
invalid request and every result other than exit status zero to
`Command_Failed`. `Spawn.Pool.Execute` also accepts a structured request with
an absolute executable, ordered arguments, a complete replacement environment,
child working directory, independent output streams and timeout. It returns
the exact exit, signal, timeout or spawn-failure result; `Execute_Checked` maps
every nonzero result to the compatibility exception.
There is no intermediate `spawn_wrapper` process.

Protocol version 1
------------------
Every socket exchange is one exact frame. Its 12-byte header contains the
ASCII magic `SPWN`, big-endian `u16` version `1`, big-endian `u16` message kind
and big-endian `u32` payload length. Message kinds are `1` for shell requests,
`2` for exec requests and `3` for results. The length excludes the header.

A string is a big-endian `u32` byte count followed by those bytes. A vector is
a big-endian `u32` element count followed by its elements. Strings retain their
exact non-NUL bytes; no text encoding or shell quoting is applied by the
protocol. Timeouts are two's-complement big-endian `i64` milliseconds, with
`-1` meaning unlimited.

The two request payloads are:

  shell = string(command), string(directory), i64(timeout)

  exec  = string(executable), u32(argc), string[argc],
          u32(envc), { string(name), string(value) }[envc],
          string(directory), stream(stdout), stream(stderr), i64(timeout)

The shell request inherits the manager environment and is normalized to
`/bin/bash -o pipefail -c command`. Compatible commands retain the predecessor
minimum of two bytes. The exec request uses an absolute
executable as implicit `argv[0]`; its transmitted arguments exclude `argv[0]`
and its environment replaces the complete inherited environment. Its working
directory is also absolute. Version 1 always connects stdin to `/dev/null`. A
stream is one mode byte: `0` selects
`/dev/null`, while `1` is followed by an absolute path string opened without
following a final symlink and with truncate semantics. An absent file is
created from mode `0666` filtered by the manager's `umask`, matching shell
redirection; truncating an existing file preserves its mode. Callers which
need an owner-only output may create it with mode `0600` before the request.

A result starts with one kind byte: `0` plus `u32` exit status, `1` plus `u16`
signal, `2` with no further field for timeout, `3` plus `u16` failure stage,
`u32` errno and a diagnostic string for a spawn failure, or `4`/`5` plus a
diagnostic string for request/protocol rejection. The complete field map,
stage numbering and bounds are maintained beside the protocol constants in
`src/spawn-protocol.ads`; golden-byte tests independently pin both request
variants and every result alternative.
Diagnostic text is truncated when necessary so the complete result remains
within the active frame bound; result kind, failure stage and errno are never
discarded to make it fit.

`Spawn.Pool.Init` requires the absolute path of the matching `spawn_manager`
binary. The library never searches `PATH`, and it passes the buffer bound and
socket address as separate arguments rather than reparsing a command string.
The bound is at least 30 bytes, the smallest legal shell request frame, and no
larger than the fixed protocol maximum.
Each pool creates its socket endpoints below one randomized mode-`0700`
directory and removes that directory during cleanup. Cleanup also accepts an
already absent private directory, for example when a caller removed its
temporary parent through the final manager request.

Lifecycle boundaries
--------------------
Each request owns a new process group. Timeouts terminate and reap the group,
and pool cleanup interrupts every manager before waiting for active leases.
The version 1 API has no independent single-request cancellation operation;
`Cleanup` cancels the complete pool.

Normal leader exit also terminates and reaps any remaining member of the
request group before its exit or signal result is returned. If that containment
cannot complete, the manager makes one final group-kill attempt and fail-stops
instead of resuming without ownership. The pool observes the lost connection
as a protocol failure and poisons every manager. This prevents background
descendants from crossing the lease boundary into a later request.

The leader remains unreaped until all request-group signals have been sent,
so its numeric process-group identity cannot be recycled into an unrelated
target. The manager stops publishing that identity to its signal handler
before the final reap.

Every request sets Linux `no_new_privs` before arming its parent-death signal.
Set-user-ID, set-group-ID and file-capability metadata therefore cannot grant
the child new privileges or clear that signal during `execve`. The restriction
is inherited by shell children and structured-exec descendants.

The parent-death signal covers the direct request leader only; Linux clears
that setting in children created by the target. Normal request completion and
catchable manager termination still clean the complete process group. If the
manager itself is lost through an uncatchable event such as `SIGKILL` or OOM,
already forked descendants require the separately selected cgroup containment
contract. Version 1 does not claim process-group-only cleanup after the process
which owns that cleanup has ceased to exist.

`Pid_Setup` continues to receive the long-lived manager PID. An optional
`Pid_Reset` runs after a valid result while the same pool lease is still
exclusive, before another caller can reuse that manager. Abuild uses the pair
to move a manager into one worker cgroup for the request and return it to the
Abuild process cgroup afterwards. If setup, transport, reset or caller task
execution fails, the complete pool is poisoned rather than exposing any
manager for reuse. A direct caller which supplies no reset retains the
manager-wide placement behavior explicitly.

Version 1 reports internal and child-spawn failures through one result kind.
Stages which can represent manager-side supervision therefore poison the
complete pool, including the ambiguous error-pipe and process-group stages.
Stages which can only be child-side pre-exec failures remain reusable.

A descendant which deliberately calls `setsid()` can leave the request process
group; containing that case requires the separately selected cgroup policy and
is not claimed by the process-group fallback.

Frame writes and completion after the first byte have a fixed monotonic
five-second deadline. For a finite child timeout, the first result byte is
bounded by that timeout plus the transport allowance. An intentionally
unlimited child also has an unlimited first-result-byte wait because version 1
does not add a heartbeat protocol.

A small C module is the only post-`fork` boundary. It blocks manager
termination signals across `fork` and process-group publication; the child
restores default `SIGINT` and `SIGTERM` dispositions before clearing its signal
mask. It then performs child-only working-directory and descriptor setup,
calls `execve`, reports pre-exec `errno`, waits for the exact status and owns
timeout cleanup.
Protocol handling, request construction, policy, diagnostics and pool state
remain Ada. Comments in the C module document the operations which are safe
between `fork` and `execve` and the ownership of every child cleanup path.
Each C function states its local contract, while the child and parent execution
paths label setup, containment, exec synchronization, wait, result and cleanup
phases explicitly.

